# =============================================================================
# ui/.prettierrc
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Purpose:
#   Prettier configuration for the CardDemo SPA, resolved by the `format`
#   (`prettier --check .`) and `format:write` scripts in ui/package.json. `format` is
#   a required step in .github/workflows/ui-ci.yml, so these values are a build
#   contract rather than an editor preference: a value that disagrees with the
#   committed tree fails that job.
# Parameters:
#   Not applicable. This file declares formatter options and takes no inputs.
# Return values:
#   Not applicable. Configuration resolution produces no application value.
# Exceptions or errors:
#   Invalid YAML prevents configuration resolution; the format gate surfaces that
#   non-zero exit rather than ignoring it.
#
# Alternatives Considered:
#   This file is YAML, not JSON. Prettier resolves an extensionless `.prettierrc` as
#   either, and JSON was rejected because strict JSON admits no comments, which would
#   move every rationale below away from the option it explains. ui/package.json shows
#   that cost directly: being strict JSON, it collects its pin rationale in a separate
#   `carddemoDependencyRationale` key.
# Alternatives Considered:
#   eslint-config-prettier and eslint-plugin-prettier are deliberately absent, so
#   formatting and linting stay independent checks. Keeping them apart means a
#   formatting disagreement can never justify relaxing a `jsdoc/require-*` rule in
#   ui/eslint.config.js, and it is safe because that config enables no stylistic rule
#   for Prettier to contradict.
# Assumptions:
#   Most values below equal Prettier's default in the pinned version and are asserted
#   anyway, because Prettier changes its own defaults across major versions -- 2.0
#   changed trailingComma, arrowParens and endOfLine, and 3.0 changed trailingComma
#   again -- so an unset option turns a dependency bump into a tree-wide reformat, and
#   can also be supplied by a contributor's editor settings instead. Options whose
#   defaults suit this tree and carry no decision are left unset.
# =============================================================================

# Trade-offs: 100 rather than Prettier's default 80, because two things here are long
# by construction. Identifiers are traceable to the COBOL records they carry
# (displayCardNumber, transactionCategoryBalance), and generic type arguments stack
# because keyset pagination is expressed as an envelope -- `Promise<PageResponse<
# CardSummary>>` is 34 characters of return type before the parameter list. At 80 those
# combine to break one typed signature across three lines. The cost is soft wrapping in
# a narrow split view, which is a per-reader viewer setting, whereas a hard wrap is
# committed into the diff for everyone.
# Alternatives Considered: 80, and 120. 80 is the default and the width the tree was
# first written to, which is precisely why it lost -- deferring to what was authored
# first ratifies an accident rather than deciding anything. 120 stops two files fitting
# side by side on a common display, and this tree is read as screen-plus-test pairs.
# Assumptions: no width can corrupt the verbatim message catalog in
# ui/src/messages/messages.ts, because Prettier never breaks a string literal. That is
# what keeps a width change clear of Transformation Rule T8's character-for-character
# message guarantee.
printWidth: 100

# Assumptions: spaces, and two of them, so one indent level occupies the same width in
# every viewer. A tab renders at whatever tab stop the reader configured, which would
# make both the indent and the remaining printWidth budget viewer-dependent.
useTabs: false
tabWidth: 2

# Assumptions: semicolons are always printed to remove the automatic-semicolon-insertion
# hazard, where a statement beginning with `(` or `[` parses as a continuation of the
# previous line. Omitting them instead relies on Prettier inserting a defensive leading
# semicolon on exactly those lines.
semi: true

# Trade-offs: this option and jsxSingleQuote below are ONE decision set to opposite
# sides, which is why neither can be justified alone. This one governs JavaScript
# literals and that one governs JSX attribute values, so in a .tsx file mixing both on
# one line a double quote is always markup (`gap="large"`) and a single quote is always
# JavaScript (`useState('')`) -- the quote character tells a reader which language the
# line is speaking. Setting both to the same side, as the default does, erases that.
# Assumptions: this changes delimiters only and can never change a string's CONTENT.
# Prettier re-quotes a literal by swapping the delimiter and adjusting escapes, so a
# verbatim message constant survives byte-identical, which is what makes the setting
# safe for a file whose whole purpose is Rule T8 fidelity. No message constant needs
# escaping either way: the COBOL constants these transcribe are themselves
# apostrophe-delimited and app/cpy carries no doubled-quote escapes, so this option
# cannot introduce a backslash into a verbatim string.
singleQuote: true

# Assumptions: JSX attribute values stay double-quoted, which is HTML's convention and
# matches ui/index.html, the document a reader meets first. See singleQuote above for
# why the two options are deliberately on opposite sides.
jsxSingleQuote: false

# Trade-offs: a trailing comma wherever valid makes appending an entry to a multi-line
# list a one-line diff instead of two -- material for the long literal tables here, such
# as the message catalog and the theme token map -- at the cost of a comma some readers
# find redundant. It is also the option Prettier has changed most, so the one most worth
# stating explicitly.
trailingComma: 'all'

# Trade-offs: a sole arrow-function parameter keeps its parentheses, so adding a type
# annotation or a second parameter changes only the text inside parentheses that already
# exist and the diff shows the change alone. The cost is slight noise on short callbacks;
# omitting them was Prettier's pre-2.0 default and was changed for this reason.
arrowParens: 'always'

# Assumptions: line endings are normalised to LF because this application is built and
# served on Linux. The failure mode prevented is a file committed with CRLF from a
# Windows checkout: it looks correct locally and fails `prettier --check .` in CI.
# Fixing it at the formatter removes the class of problem rather than depending on every
# contributor's git autocrlf setting.
endOfLine: 'lf'
